# Line endings.
#
# Development happens on Windows, CI runs on Linux. Without a rule here, whichever
# endings a file happens to be saved with go into the repository, and a tool that
# rewrites a file with native endings turns a three-line change into a whole-file
# diff. Git's own conversion is also machine-wide on this box (core.autocrlf=true
# lives in Program Files), so behaviour would otherwise depend on who cloned it.

# Default: store everything as LF in the repository, check out native on each
# platform. This is the safe baseline for source.
* text=auto

# Must be LF. These run on Linux inside GitHub Actions, and CRLF in a workflow or a
# shell script fails there in ways that read as a confusing CI error rather than as
# a line-ending problem.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf

# Must be CRLF. Windows batch files are parsed line by line by cmd.exe, and LF
# endings can break labels and goto in ways that only show up at run time.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Inno Setup script: Windows-only tooling, keep it native to the platform it runs on.
*.iss text eol=crlf

# Binary. Never convert these; conversion corrupts them silently.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.exe binary
*.dll binary
*.wav binary
*.mp3 binary
*.ogg binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
